    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first settlers of St. Nazianz numbered 113 people who traveled from the Black Forest of Baden, Germany.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Nazianz%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Father Ambrose Oschwald founded the colony after purchasing nearly four thousand acres of land in Manitowoc County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Nazianz%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlers reached the site of St. Nazianz by traveling from Manitowoc by oxcart and cutting through dense forest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Nazianz%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village of St. Nazianz was named in 1896, fifteen years after the Society of the Divine Savior was founded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
